%I A396280 #24 Jul 27 2026 04:28:52
%S A396280 2,8,32,128,432,1024,2000,3456,5488,8192,11664,16000,21296,27648,
%T A396280 35152,43904,54000,65536,78608,93312,109744,128000,148176,170368,
%U A396280 194672,221184,250000,281216,314928,351232,390224,432000,476656,524288,574992,628864,686000,746496,810448
%N A396280 a(1) = 2, a(2) = 8, a(3) = 32; a(n) = 16*(n-2)^3 for n >= 4.
%C A396280 a(n) is the total number of coordinate entries in the n-th cyclic square, a combinatorial array formed by merging cyclic Latin squares of order A019442(n) cell-wise, with each cell holding a tuple whose length is the number of layers; see Bassey (2026). If order(n) = A019442(n) and L(n) is the number of layers, then a(n) = order(n)^2 * L(n). For n = 1, 2, 3 (orders 1, 2, 4) L(n) = 2 exceptionally; for n >= 4 (order = 4*(n-2)) L(n) = order(n)/4, giving a(n) = 16*(n-2)^3.
%C A396280 For n >= 4, a(n) = A033431(2*(n-2)); the sequence departs from this scaling only in the three exceptional leading terms.
%D A396280 B. G. Bassey, Cyclic Squares: The Discovery of a New Combinatorial Structure, First Edition, Glasgow, United Kingdom, 2026. ISBN 9798198234383.
%H A396280 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A396280/b396280.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A396280 <a href="/index/Rec#order_04">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (4,-6,4,-1).
%F A396280 a(n) = 16*(n-2)^3 for n >= 4, a(1) = 2, a(2) = 8, a(3) = 32.
%F A396280 For n >= 4: a(n) = A033431(2*(n-2)) = 2*(2*(n-2))^3.
%F A396280 a(n) = 4*a(n-1) - 6*a(n-2) + 4*a(n-3) - a(n-4) for n >= 8.
%F A396280 G.f.: 2*x*(1+6*x^2 + 20*x^3 + 41*x^4 - 28*x^5 + 8*x^6)/(1 - x)^4. - _Stefano Spezia_, Jul 21 2026
%e A396280 a(5) = 432: the 5th term of A019442 is 12, so order(5) = 12 with L(5) = 12/4 = 3 layers, giving 12^2 * 3 = 432 total entries -- the (12 X 12)_3 cyclic square of Table 6.
%e A396280 a(4) = 128: order(4) = 8, L(4) = 8/4 = 2, giving 8^2 * 2 = 128.
%t A396280 Join[{2, 8, 32}, 16*Range[2, 40]^3] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Jul 27 2026 *)
%o A396280 (PARI) a(n) = if(n==1, 2, if(n==2, 8, if(n==3, 32, 16*(n-2)^3)));
%Y A396280 Cf. A019442 (the orders), A033431 (a(n) = 2*n^3; this sequence's tail for n >= 4 is a subsequence of it), A000578 (the cubes).
%K A396280 nonn,easy,changed
%O A396280 1,1
%A A396280 _Bassey Godwin Bassey_, Jul 20 2026
